  "analysis": "Strange: df reports 960G used / 0 avail, but du only finds ~179M across /usr, /var, /opt, /root, /home. The bulk of reported usage must be elsewhere (large files, deleted-but-open files, or another mount). I need to locate the space hogs before apt can download 70MB of archives.",
  "plan": "1. List all mounts and disk usage. 2. Find large files (>100M) across the filesystem. 3. Find the largest top-level directories with du. This will reveal where to reclaim space.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "df -h 2>&1\n",
      "duration": 0.3
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "du -h --max-depth=1 / 2>/dev/null | sort -h | tail -20\n",
      "duration": 3.0
    }
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "The df reports 959G used but du only finds 179M actually present on disk — the device is a shared overlay and df's free-space accounting is unreliable for the apt check. The apt free-space check is what failed. The patch history itself shows the working workaround: `apt-get -o Acquire::Check-Free-Space=false install -y gcc make wget`. The AVOID entries (asciinema recording, /dev/shm writable tests) are unrelated traps I will not run. I'll use the Check-Free-Space=false workaround to install the toolchain.",
  "plan": "1. Install gcc, make, wget with Acquire::Check-Free-Space=false to bypass the misleading free-space check. 2. Verify gcc/gcov/make/wget are present. Then proceed to download and compile SQLite with gcov instrumentation.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "apt-get -o Acquire::Check-Free-Space=false install -y gcc make wget 2>&1 | tail -8\n",
      "duration": 60.0
    }
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "df reports 0 available everywhere, yet du only accounts for 179M on a 984G volume. This mismatch typically means space is consumed by deleted-but-still-open files in the overlay, or the free-space accounting is broken. The `-o Acquire::Check-Free-Space=false` did not defeat the check, so apt still aborts. I need to (a) determine if writes actually work and (b) find what's eating space. The AVOID history entries (asciinema recording, /dev/shm tests, /app/test.bin dd) are unrelated traps and must not be run.",
  "plan": "1. Check inodes and look for deleted files held open by processes (common overlay space leak). 2. Test whether writing a file actually succeeds on disk. 3. Based on results, either free space or bypass apt's check. I will not run the AVOID commands.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "df -i / 2>&1\n",
      "duration": 0.3
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "lsof 2>/dev/null | grep -i deleted | head -20\n",
      "duration": 1.0
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "dd if=/dev/zero of=/tmp/space_test bs=1M count=50 2>&1; echo EXIT=$?; rm -f /tmp/space_test\n",
      "duration": 1.0
    }
  ]
}

